<test>
    <!-- Aggregations that return every group of a large result through the merge of the adaptive aggregator.
         The merge converts each of its 256 buckets in units of about 16,384 groups, and all the merge threads
         hand their chunks to one processor, which forwards them one at a time. So the merge squashes the
         chunks of the units of a bucket into chunks of at least `max_block_size` rows. The queries return 20
         and 50 million groups, 78 and 195 thousand per bucket, which the units alone would output in several
         chunks per bucket. The squashing copies the rows of the units that share a chunk, and the waiting it
         saves grows with the number of merge threads, so on few cores the queries mostly check the cost of
         the copy. ClickBench does not measure this: its queries with many groups return 10 rows. -->
    <query>SELECT WatchID, count() FROM hits_100m_single WHERE intHash32(WatchID) % 100 &lt; 20 GROUP BY WatchID FORMAT Null</query>
    <query>SELECT WatchID, count() FROM hits_100m_single WHERE intHash32(WatchID) % 100 &lt; 50 GROUP BY WatchID FORMAT Null</query>
</test>
